// Package runtime is the code every package generated by schemagen shares.
//
// A generated package is the part of the code that depends on the schemas: the
// types, the per-type methods, the schema nodes and the compiled patterns. What
// is the same for every schema -- the in-place JSON decoder, the one-pass
// encoder, the identity of a value, the exact number core, the "format"
// checkers, the schema evaluator -- lives here, once, and generated code imports
// it as rt:
//
//	import rt "github.com/mgilbir/schemagen/runtime"
//
// # Getting it
//
// The module is versioned with schemagen: a release vX.Y.Z of schemagen is
// paired with runtime/vX.Y.Z of this module. A module that contains generated
// code requires it like any other dependency:
//
//	go get github.com/mgilbir/schemagen/runtime@latest
//
// It depends on the standard library, github.com/mgilbir/goecma262 (the
// ECMA-262 regular expression engine "pattern" is defined by) and
// golang.org/x/net/idna (the hostname formats). It does not depend on schemagen
// itself, on its command line, or on anything else.
//
// # What it promises
//
// The exported API exists for generated code. It is documented and it is
// stable, but it is not a library to write against: the shapes of Doc, Span and
// the rest are whatever generated code needs them to be. Within a major
// version the API only grows -- a name, a signature or a behaviour generated
// code depends on does not change -- so a runtime newer than the schemagen that
// generated a package always serves it, and a package can be regenerated
// without changing the runtime it builds against, or the runtime updated
// without regenerating.
//
// The other direction is the one that can fail. Code generated by a newer
// schemagen may use API an older runtime does not have, and every generated
// file says so on its first line of code: it declares
//
//	var _ rt.API1
//
// where the number is the API level the generator emitted for. An older runtime
// does not declare that type, and the build stops at that line with
// "undefined: rt.API1" rather than at whichever call happens to come first; the
// fix is the go get above. A runtime never lowers its level, so a level is a
// promise about every name up to it.
package runtime

// API1 is the first API level of this runtime. Generated code declares a
// variable of the level it needs, so that a runtime too old for it fails to
// compile at one recognisable line. See the package documentation.
type API1 struct{}
